1. d4 g6 2. c4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. e4 Nc6 5. d5 Nd4 6. Be3 e5 ({More common here
is} 6... c5 {although I was familiar with the game Seirawan-Keene, Netherlands
1982 which continued} 7. Nge2 Qb6 8. Nxd4 cxd4 9. Na4 Qa5+ 10. Bd2 Qc7 11. c5
$1 dxc5 12. Bb5+ Bd7 13. Bxd7+ Qxd7 14. Nxc5 $16) 7. Nge2 Nxe2 8. Bxe2 Nh6 $6 (
{Simply} 8... Ne7 {was preferable, e.g.} 9. g4 f5 10. gxf5 gxf5 11. Bh5+ Kf8)
9. g4 $1 Qh4 $2 ({This queen sortie must be incorrect but Black understandably
did not want to resign himself to} 9... f6 {when White has a large spatial
advantage with opportunities for attack on both the kingside and queenside})
10. Rg1 $1 f6 {The h-pawn cannot be taken because of 11 Kd2! but now the black
queen is somewhat out on a limb, allowing White to quickly begin his queenside
offensive.} 11. Nb5 Kd8 12. c5 $1 {White must act quickly before Black has a
chance to play ...Nf7 and ...Bh6, exchanging his poor bishop and leaving White
weak on the dark squares.} Nf7 ({Or if} 12... a6 {probably} 13. Na3 {to answer}
dxc5 {with} 14. d6 $1 {when the newly-formed weakness at b6 is apparent.}) 13.
Qc2 Bd7 ({The best chance. Not, of course,} 13... a6 $2 14. cxd6 {etc.}) 14. c6
$5 ({I saw} 14. cxd6 cxd6 15. Qc7+ Ke7 ({during the game I thought that Black
could put up some resistance with} 15... Ke8 16. Nxd6+ (16. Qxb7 {may be better
}) 16... Nxd6 17. Qxd6 Bf8 18. Qc7 Bb4+ {however this line is simpler than
what I played, and seems to give a clearly winning position.}) 16. Nxd6 $1 {
etc,}) 14... bxc6 15. dxc6 Be6 {This natural move meets with a strong reply,
but the alternatives of 15...Bc8 and 15...Be8 hardly appear appetising} 16. Bc4
$1 {This positionally-motivated move leads to a forced variation which
culminates in the winning of Black's c7 pawn.} Re8 ({Not} 16... Bxc4 $2 17.
Qxc4 {and White's queen penetrates to e6 and delivers mate.}) 17. Bxe6 Rxe6 18.
Qb3 ({Only now did I observe that my intended} 18. Nxc7 Kxc7 19. Qb3 {fails to}
Nd8) 18... Re7 19. Nxa7 Ke8 ({Black's only defence to save his rook against 20
Qb7 comes anyway. No prizes for} 19... Rxa7 $4 20. Qb8#) 20. Qb7 Rd8 21. Nc8
Re6 22. Qxc7 ({Also possible was} 22. Qb3 {forcing} d5 23. exd5 Red6 24. Nxd6+
Nxd6 {when Black can struggle on. The text is stronger as the pawn is going
all the way home.}) 22... Bh6 $1 {Finally this bishop sees the light of dawn
and I suddenly noticed that my intended 23 Bb6 did not threaten mate in one,
as I had previously assumed, for some inexplicable reason.} 23. O-O-O {White
still has to be careful, and I had to have a long think before playing this
move, to convince myself that I was still winning. If White's king stays on e1
there is a danger of ...Nf7-g5-f3+ causing difficulties.} Qxf2 $1 (23... Bxe3+
$1 {Fritz}) 24. Bxh6 Nxh6 25. Kb1 $1 {This safe and strong move ensures the
win, as White can always put a rook on c1 to support the push of the c-pawn,
the King is also now away from annoying checks.} Nf7 26. Qb7 1-0

